WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.048 --> 00:00:03.896
People have always looked up at the moon with wonder.

2
00:00:03.897 --> 00:00:11.548
Ancient people worshipped the moon as a goddess, and many believed that she was responsible for the creation of time.

3
00:00:11.549 --> 00:00:20.366
The first calendars were based on the phases of the moon, which change consistently to complete a full cycle every 28 days.

4
00:00:20.367 --> 00:00:28.611
In the moon’s bright features, people have imagined a human face, a rabbit, and even a round piece of cheese. It

5
00:00:28.612 --> 00:00:40.354
was not until the twentieth century, however, that the United States and the Soviet Union began to develop technology that would allow humans to visit the alluring white sphere. Both

6
00:00:40.355 --> 00:00:49.681
countries were set on conquering outer space; both wanted to prove their scientific superiority and their military strength. This

7
00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:55.672
"space race," as it became known, served to fuel people’s interest in the moon. The

8
00:00:55.673 --> 00:01:05.492
first major hurdle to space travel was overcome by the Soviet Union in 1957 with the launch of the first artificial satellite. Sputnik,

9
00:01:05.493 --> 00:01:10.736
as it was called, was catapulted into orbit by a rocket. It

10
00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:19.905
weighed about 83 kilograms and looked like a giant ball with several antennas that protruded out like whiskers on a cat’s nose. It

11
00:01:19.906 --> 00:01:30.318
also had two radio transmitters attached to its spherical body that sent out signals, helping Soviet scientists learn more about the density of space. After completing

12
00:01:30.319 --> 00:01:38.550
its task, Sputnik was brought back toward the earth, but was destroyed upon reentry into the atmosphere. Despite the

13
00:01:38.551 --> 00:01:44.469
loss of Sputnik, the experiment was considered a great success. As a

14
00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:53.454
result, the Soviets attempted several more satellite launches, including one that took a dog, named Laika, up into space. Laika became

15
00:01:53.455 --> 00:01:58.461
the first living creature from Earth to travel into orbit. The first

16
00:01:58.462 --> 00:02:05.714
human to leave Earth’s atmosphere was also part of the Soviet team, Colonel Yuri Gagarin. In 1961,

17
00:02:05.715 --> 00:02:12.442
Gagarin was chosen to command a space capsule called Vostok 1 . It

18
00:02:12.443 --> 00:02:17.520
blasted off from a secret launch pad located in Soviet Kazakhstan. It

19
00:02:17.521 --> 00:02:25.432
traveled a complete circle around the earth in just 108 minutes. Then, it returned to the ground over Russia. Before it

20
00:02:25.433 --> 00:02:30.964
landed, Gagarin ejected from the capsule and parachuted to safety. After this

21
00:02:30.965 --> 00:02:37.763
adventurous ride, many Soviets started to call Gagarin "the Columbus of the Cosmos." Meanwhile, the

22
00:02:37.764 --> 00:02:44.676
United States was trying desperately to match the success of the Soviet space program. Unfortunately, their

23
00:02:44.677 --> 00:02:51.572
first attempt to launch a satellite had ended in an explosion on the launch pad. But this

24
00:02:51.573 --> 00:03:03.728
did not deter the Americans, and soon after Gagarin’s historic flight, they launched their own manned mission, Freedom 7 , carrying Alan Shepard into space. Shepard,

25
00:03:03.729 --> 00:03:13.564
who had been a navy test pilot, earned the distinction of being the first astronaut to return safely with his ship without having to use a parachute. This

26
00:03:13.565 --> 00:03:20.388
inspired a second mission, Friendship 7 , piloted by astronaut John Glenn.

27
00:03:20.389 --> 00:03:33.457
With this limited amount of manned space experience, American President John F. Kennedy announced that his country would embark on a new space program managed by their aeronautics agency, NASA. He

28
00:03:33.458 --> 00:03:43.600
made a promise to the American public that, before the end of the decade, the country would send an astronaut to the moon and return him to the earth unharmed. This

29
00:03:43.601 --> 00:03:50.449
program was named Apollo after the Greek god of the sun, and work began immediately. The

30
00:03:50.450 --> 00:04:01.518
initial stage was to determine how NASA engineers should build a space shuttle that would reach the moon while keeping costs low and minimizing risk to human life. After

31
00:04:01.519 --> 00:04:10.179
much debate, they decided to focus on constructing an Apollo spacecraft that consisted of four main sections. The

32
00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:19.831
Command Module was the part where the astronauts would sit when the spacecraft was launched and where they would spend most of their time while on board. The Service

33
00:04:19.832 --> 00:04:26.163
Module contained all of their equipment, such as oxygen tanks and the engine. The Lunar

34
00:04:26.164 --> 00:04:32.400
Module was a small part of the ship that could detach from the main spacecraft. It was

35
00:04:32.401 --> 00:04:36.008
the section that would actually land on the moon. The final

36
00:04:36.009 --> 00:04:43.643
section, the Launch Escape Tower, would be used during the launch and would be discarded shortly after takeoff. Altogether, the

37
00:04:43.644 --> 00:04:51.318
four sections fit together to look like a long, thin rocket with a wide, round tailpipe. For several

38
00:04:51.319 --> 00:04:57.730
years, engineers worked hard to design a fully functioning Apollo spacecraft. In the

39
00:04:57.731 --> 00:05:01.885
beginning, the program suffered some setbacks. During a

40
00:05:01.886 --> 00:05:09.790
test of Apollo 1 , an explosion resulted in a fire that killed three astronauts on the launch pad. This

41
00:05:09.791 --> 00:05:20.293
was devastating to the program, so engineers decided to focus on testing unmanned flights until they were certain that no more astronauts would be harmed. After six

42
00:05:20.294 --> 00:05:28.873
major tests, in 1968, the Americans were ready to allow astronauts to pilot spacecraft again. Human-piloted Apollo

43
00:05:28.874 --> 00:05:39.360
7 circled the moon in October of that year, and three more manned missions were launched to test various sections of the spacecraft. Then, in

44
00:05:39.361 --> 00:05:45.262
1969, NASA announced that it was prepared to reach the moon. Commander Neil

45
00:05:45.263 --> 00:05:52.334
Armstrong, Michael Collins, and Buzz Aldrin were chosen for the mission, Apollo 11 . Like

46
00:05:52.335 --> 00:06:00.168
Alan Shepard, Armstrong was a navy test pilot with a lot of experience flying experimental airplanes. He

47
00:06:00.169 --> 00:06:07.755
had previously worked on other American space missions and was known to cope well with periods of extreme stress. Armstrong

48
00:06:07.756 --> 00:06:19.148
would command the crew, Collins would pilot the Command Module (called Columbia ), and Aldrin would be responsible for piloting the Lunar Module (called the Eagle ).

49
00:06:19.149 --> 00:06:28.078
Apollo 11 was launched from the Kennedy Space Center in Florida at 1:32 pm on July 16, 1969.

50
00:06:28.079 --> 00:06:32.305
After four days of travel, it began orbiting the moon.

51
00:06:32.306 --> 00:06:40.052
When the spacecraft was in position, Aldrin and Armstrong climbed into the Eagle and separated from Columbia

52
00:06:40.053 --> 00:06:44.629
. "The Eagle has wings," Armstrong reported to ground

53
00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:50.214
control. The descent to the moon took almost three hours and the landing was full of

54
00:06:50.215 --> 00:06:57.475
tension. The Eagle was coming in too fast, and the flight controllers back on Earth thought the astronauts would have to

55
00:06:57.476 --> 00:07:01.368
abort. Armstrong saw that they were heading for a field of

56
00:07:01.369 --> 00:07:07.858
boulders. He took manual control of the craft and began skimming across the surface to find a better

57
00:07:07.859 --> 00:07:16.446
spot. Moments later, with only thirty seconds of fuel remaining, the Eagle’s footpads touched the moon’s surface in the Sea of Tranquility.

58
00:07:16.447 --> 00:07:25.687
From inside the Eagle , Buzz Aldrin confirmed the successful touchdown with the words, "Tranquility base. The Eagle has

59
00:07:25.688 --> 00:07:32.336
landed." After several hours, Armstrong crawled out of the hatch and climbed down the outside

60
00:07:32.337 --> 00:07:42.587
ladder. From the bottom rung, he jumped the last three feet down to the moon and declared: "That’s one small step for man, one giant leap for

61
00:07:42.588 --> 00:07:48.419
mankind." With these words, Neil Armstrong became the first person to walk on the

62
00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:55.401
moon. First, Armstrong took photographs and soil samples in case the mission had to be aborted

63
00:07:55.402 --> 00:08:03.250
suddenly. Then, he and Aldrin, who had climbed down after him, jumped across the landscape and planted the American

64
00:08:03.251 --> 00:08:10.481
flag. They also unveiled a plaque that read, "Here men from the planet Earth first set foot upon the Moon.

65
00:08:10.482 --> 00:08:16.556
July 1969 AD. We came in peace for all mankind."

66
00:08:16.557 --> 00:08:23.876
When Apollo 11 returned safely to Earth on July 24, the astronauts were welcomed as heroes.

67
00:08:23.877 --> 00:08:29.374
Kennedy’s promise had been fulfilled and NASA had achieved a miraculous feat.

68
00:08:29.375 --> 00:08:34.873
Ancient people probably never imagined that a human would ever walk on the moon.

69
00:08:34.874 --> 00:08:42.872
But, Apollo 11 turned distant dreams into reality, bringing the ancient moon goddess firmly within grasp.

